 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "credits",
   "html": "<p>Course: SCI-6476 Transformable Design Methods</p><p>Instructor: Chuck Hoberman</p>",
   "layout": "credits"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "quote",
   "layout": "quote",
   "html": "<h2>A tool that lets a structure move before it is built.</h2>"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.305,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<h3>Abstract</h3><p>This project is part of the development process of prismo, a transformable lamp based on the prismatic structure. This parametric tool provides convenience for users to intuitively edit 1 DOF prismatic structure’s aggregation and simulate its movement in real time. With the help of this tool, I was able to iterate multiple designs and test their movement purely in the digital environment, which greatly speeds up the development process.</p><p><a href=\"/work/prismo/\">See more about the Prismo lamp →</a></p>",
       "kind": "text"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.695,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "transform/transform_01.jpg",
       "width": 2400,
       "height": 1600,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#342008",
       "kind": "photo"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "section",
   "layout": "section",
   "html": "<h2>Polar Array Design</h2>"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.3751,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "loop",
       "src": "/media/transform/transform_02.mp4",
       "width": 600,
       "height": 600,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#958472",
       "kind": "small"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.6249,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "transform/transform_03.jpg",
       "width": 2400,
       "height": 1441,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#ffffff",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.295,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<h3>Problem</h3><p>The conventional workflow working with prismatic structure is labor-intensive and time-consuming: Noticing the low efficiency, I realized there is a strong need for a digital parametric tool that could be used to accelerate the design iteration process.<br>The tool should enable designers to design and simulate movement in the digital environment to freely (and more efficiently) explore more potential opportunities.</p>",
       "kind": "text"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.705,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "transform/transform_04.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fefefe",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.625,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "transform/transform_05.jpg",
       "width": 1442,
       "height": 740,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#afbde1",
       "kind": "photo"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.375,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<h3>Solution</h3><p>Since the goal of this tool is to help people design, it has to be user-friendly and intuitive to use. I decided to use a modular component to represent each unit. This software architecture allows users directly see the connection between individual input parameters and output geometry.</p>",
       "kind": "text"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "video",
       "src": "/media/transform/transform_06.mp4",
       "poster": "/media/transform/transform_06.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1084,
       "audio": false,
       "kind": "video"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "video",
       "src": "/media/transform/transform_07.mp4",
       "poster": "/media/transform/transform_07.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1084,
       "audio": false,
       "kind": "video"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.375,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "html": "<h3>Challenge</h3><p>The challenge is to understand the math behind the transformation: The input angle and output angle do not map linearly. At the first attempt, I called intersect functions from RhinoCommon as a hard way to solve the geometrical equation. It works, but the intersection method is a bit clumsy computation-wise.</p><p>I further broke down the geometry relationships into trigonometric equations. This change not only simplify the code and also increase computational efficiency.</p>",
       "kind": "text"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.625,
     "blocks": [
